worked this out on my own .. Essentially in the first step, reverse the full Array and in the second step either skip the 0th or the last element
for the array  reversal. For right rotate, skip 0th, for left rotate skip the last (think of the direction of movement to remember this)

let SIZE be the array size.

RightRotate(1, Array) {
    Rotate(Array, 0, Array.length - 1);
    Rotate(Array, 1, Array.length - 1);
}

LeftRotate(1, Array) {
    Rotate(Array, 0, Array.length - 1);
    Rotate(Array, 0, Array.length - 2);
}

void Rotate(Array, startOffset, endOffset) {
  int size = endOffset - startOffset + 1;

  /** theres no data dependency in this loop */
  parallel_for (int counter = 0; counter < size / 2; counter++) {
      swap( Array[startOffset + counter], Array[endOffset - counter]);
  }
}


..... Pretty cool eh ... check it out for yourself essentially take an array do the swaps across the middle element
once done repeat after ignoring the zeroeth element. Result is a right rotate by 1. An operation like this will be cpu
bound and have good dcache footprint... exactly where we want it.

Q: How does one scale out?
A: Simple split your array into chunks do the rotations internally then in the next stage pick the boundary [1]
   elements of each chunk and do the rotations about them.

[1] A  B  C     D  E  F
 to do a right rotate do it individually
    
    C  A  B     F  D  E
now pick the left boundary of each chunk and do a right rotate
    F  A  B     C  D  E
 
for left rotate pick the right boundary. Kapeesh? :-)


    
